    "id": "news-1785092302733-6",
    "title": "AfDB Warns of $10-20 Billion Loss in Africa from Intense El Niño",
    "original_title": "Afrika oo wajahaysa khasaare dhaqaale oo gaaraya $10–20 bilyan sababo la xiriira El Niño xooggan",
    "slug": "afdb-warns-of-10-20-billion-loss-in-africa-from-intense-el-ni-o",
    "summary": "The African Development Bank has forecast that a strong El Niño event could cause economic damage across Africa ranging from $10 billion to $20 billion, with the hardest-hit nations facing GDP losses of 1-2%.",
    "content": "The African Development Bank (AfDB) has issued a warning that a powerful El Niño weather pattern, often termed 'Super El Niño,' could lead to significant economic losses for African countries. According to the bank’s Director for Climate Change and Green Growth, Anthony Nyong, the financial impact is estimated to fall between $10 billion and $20 billion. This projection stems from the expected damage to infrastructure, agriculture, and livelihoods across the continent.\n\nMr. Nyong explained that nations most severely affected could see their gross domestic product shrink by an average of 1 to 2 percent. The extreme weather conditions associated with this El Niño are anticipated to include severe droughts in some regions and devastating floods and storms in others. These events are likely to trigger widespread displacement and compound existing humanitarian challenges.\n\nThe warning comes as scientists observe a strengthening El Niño phase in the Pacific Ocean, which historically disrupts weather patterns globally. For Africa, this often translates to erratic rainfall, crop failures, and increased food insecurity. The AfDB's assessment highlights the vulnerability of economies that rely heavily on rain-fed agriculture and have limited capacity to adapt to sudden climatic shocks.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this forecast is particularly concerning. Many diaspora members maintain close family and economic ties to Somalia and other East African nations, which are frequently on the front line of El Niño-related impacts. Droughts and floods in the Horn of Africa can lead to crop loss, water shortages, and population movements, affecting remittance-dependent families and potential business investments. The AfDB's data underscores the need for proactive adaptation measures and climate-resilient infrastructure to safeguard both local communities and the diaspora’s contributions.\n\nIn response to the looming threat, the AfDB has called for increased investment in early warning systems, disaster preparedness, and climate-smart agriculture. The bank also emphasized the importance of international cooperation to mitigate the long-term economic and humanitarian consequences of such extreme weather events.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/26/afrika-oo-wajahaysa-khasaare-dhaqaale-oo-gaaraya-10-20-bilyan-sababo-la-xiriira-el-nino-xooggan/",
