    "id": "news-1785070772767-2",
    "title": "Puntland Warns Federal Military Movements in Galkayo Risk Instability",
    "original_title": "Puntland warns over Somalia federal military moves in Galkayo after Bosaso clashes",
    "slug": "puntland-warns-federal-military-movements-in-galkayo-risk-instability",
    "summary": "Puntland authorities have raised concerns over federal troop deployments near Galkayo, warning they could undermine regional stability and threaten Puntland's governance.",
    "content": "Authorities in Somalia's semi-autonomous Puntland state have expressed alarm over what they describe as military maneuvers by the federal government in the central town of Galkayo. In a statement released on Saturday, Puntland officials said they are monitoring the situation closely and warned that the deployments risk provoking conflict and destabilizing the region.\n\nThe federal government has not publicly commented on the troop movements, but Puntland accuses President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud's administration of using military and political pressure to weaken Puntland's governing institutions. The area around Galkayo, a contested town in Mudug region, has long been a flashpoint between Puntland and neighboring Galmudug state.\n\nTensions have escalated after recent clashes in Bosaso, the commercial capital of Puntland, involving federal forces and local security personnel. Puntland authorities view the federal military moves in Galkayo as a follow-up to those incidents, aimed at expanding central government influence in the region. The autonomous state has maintained its own security forces and administration since 1998, resisting full integration with Mogadishu.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments highlight the ongoing fragility of federal-state relations in Somalia. Many diaspora members trace their origins to Puntland, and instability there could affect family connections and investment flows. The standoff also underscores broader challenges in Somalia's state-building process, including disputes over resource control and security arrangements.\n\nObservers warn that continued friction between Mogadishu and regional states like Puntland could undermine progress toward political stability. The situation in Galkayo remains tense, with both sides calling for de-escalation but offering little compromise.\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/puntland-warns-somalia-federal-military-moves-galkayo-bosaso-clashes/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/puntland-warns-somalia-federal-military-moves-galkayo-bosaso-clashes/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Politics",
